Z||000000||DaveL||02-24-2000||08:59 AM||||I still haven't gotten any reply to my question about the unused black ground cable hanging from the left deck lid hinge, as you stand behind the car and lokk at the engine compartment. There is a heavy guage black ground that goes over to the block of this 87 SE 2.5 DIS, near the temp sender. There is another smaller guage black wire hanging from the same hinge. Anyone out there have an 87 SE 2.5 DIS??? Where do both leads go. If I can't get an answer,I may just hook it up to myself and fire the sucker up....||||reg||10
Z||000001||deceler8||02-24-2000||09:15 AM||||Believe that has something to do with the RFI shield attached to the rear decklid...prevents radio interference .<P>I believe it's there as the plastic decklid won't stop radio interference from the ignition as a metal decklid would.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||02-24-2000||09:35 AM||||Was it '87 or '88 when the RFI shield was built into the decklid? You would still have the wire, but it would plug into the decklid, rather than attach to a metal plate (glued?) to the bottom of the decklid.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||02-24-2000||09:54 AM||||i seem to remember a ground wire attaching to the hinge on the decklid of my 86.5 gt.  as for what its for...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-24-2000||10:44 AM||||I'm assuming one is for the EMI/RFI shield on the deck lid the other is a gound from the chassis to the engine block. GM vehicles use what is commonly called a "floating ground" The large ground wire helps cut down interference from the engine. On some cars if it isn't attached you can hear a "whine" on the radio (low volume) that varies with engine RPM. The noise is the alternator as it works. This can also cause your car to "act up" if it interferes with the ECM signals. Sometime you have to make the wire even bigger if you are installing a large stereo system. On one stereo install we had all the grounds from the stereo tied to the same spot on the chassis to cut out engine noise. (I used to install stereo's and alarm systems for a living).||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-24-2000||07:52 PM||||sounds like the deck sheild wire to me. you can't miss the sheild if it's there. 87 does still have it on all of them as far as I know. some made late in 87 might have the thing integrated in the lid. I've never seen the intergrated one. They might not need the wire.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||02-25-2000||12:59 AM||||From all I've heard only 88's had the integrated sheilding.  it was basically a "mat" like fiberglass that was just molded into the decklid.  Pontiac even got an award from it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ozzy||02-25-2000||01:06 AM||||I've got that wire on my '88 coupe. Just noticed it the other day pulling the valve cover. One end of the large wire has a large grounding lug like the two grounded to the block near the temp sender.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
